Subject: [PATCH] Add engine RND rewrite; complete notebooks 3 and 4

Reviewed the engine_v2 pipeline end to end and rebuilt the serving layer in
a new "engine RND/" folder. The two-stage architecture is unchanged and
sound; the problems were in the code and process around it.

engine RND/
  - ANALYSIS.md: full study of the v2 workflow with ~30 findings and evidence
  - recommender.py: nine numbered serving fixes, notably
      * candidates are excluded before the per-artist cap, not after
        (v2 starved heavy listeners of any artist whose top 10 they had heard)
      * the artist shortlist widens x2/x4/x8 before falling back to popularity
      * zero-reaction tracks are reachable (2,410 of 14,843 were not)
      * candidates without a track embedding are scored, not dropped
      * per-response diversity cap plus stateless session damping
      * live reactions blend into a known user's trained profile
  - artifacts.py: validated loading, v3 bundle and legacy v2 layouts
  - tools/torch_pickle.py: reads .pt state dicts as numpy without torch,
    which recovers the artist bias v2 trained with but never exported
  - tools/build_params.py: v2 exports -> validated 11 MB bundle, no pickles,
    dropping scikit-learn from the serving venv
  - tools/benchmark.py: v2's exact serving algorithm vs the new one
  - evaluation.py: leakage-free split protocol and metrics
  - 99 tests (v1 and v2 have none)

Measured over 300 users x 20 requests: distinct artists per session 11.25
-> 14.48, catalogue coverage 12.86% -> 14.07%, popularity fallbacks 0.07%
-> 0.00%, reachable tracks 12,024 -> 14,291.

notebooks/3.datacleaning-EDA.ipynb: rewritten in a plain, concise style with
no emoji, and Part B completed - real EDA on the derived artifacts covering
sparsity, the popularity long tail, Lorenz/Gini, catalogue sizes and
coverage gaps, with each measurement tied to a design decision in Chapter 4.

notebooks/4.recommendation-engine.ipynb: written from empty. Documents the
v2 pipeline, verifies the model composition against the shipped weights,
reproduces the serving algorithm on real artifacts to demonstrate the
candidate-collection and reachability defects, and explains why the
published evaluation numbers cannot be used as stated.

